[2026-07-20T13:59:30.335Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=e777b63038820381ca4b383c2a112a16] === Summary content (2 topics) ===
[2026-07-20T13:59:30.335Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=e777b63038820381ca4b383c2a112a16] Topic: Create projects from home page prompt and open new editor
[2026-07-20T13:59:30.335Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=e777b63038820381ca4b383c2a112a16]   Trigger:   The user wanted the home page prompt box (and template cards) to actually create a project and take the user straight into a new editor screen matching a provided mockup, instead of just being decorative UI.
[2026-07-20T13:59:30.335Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=e777b63038820381ca4b383c2a112a16]   Response:  - Added a `projects` table to the backend with the same row-level security and access grants pattern as the existing `profiles` table, so each signed-in user can only see and modify their own projects (columns include id, user_id, name, prompt, status, timestamps).
- Built a projects repository (`lib/projects.ts`) that keeps all database calls out of the UI: `createProject` generates the project id on the device itself for instant navigation, derives a short project name from the prompt, and saves the prompt; `getProject` fetches a single project scoped to the current user.
- Wired the home page (`app/(app)/page.tsx`) so the prompt textarea (including a keyboard shortcut to submit) and all four template cards call a shared `startProject` action that creates a project and navigates to a new editor page, with loading and error states and a redirect to sign-in for signed-out users.
- Built a brand-new full-screen editor route (`app/editor/[projectId]/page.tsx`) with a top bar, a chat panel showing the user's real prompt plus a placeholder AI response and generation checklist, and a preview canvas area, matching the provided design mockup.
[2026-07-20T13:59:30.335Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=e777b63038820381ca4b383c2a112a16]   Decisions: - **Generate the project identifier on the user's device instead of waiting on the server**: this lets the app navigate to the new project screen immediately after the request is sent, rather than waiting for a round trip, making project creation feel instant.
- **Mirror the existing user-data table's access rules exactly**: rather than designing new security rules from scratch, the new projects data was locked down with the same "only see your own rows" policy already proven out for user profiles, reducing risk and design time.
- **Keep the editor page outside the main app's navigation shell**: the editor was placed as its own full-screen route without the app's sidebar, so it can present a focused, mockup-accurate workspace instead of being squeezed into the standard app layout.
[2026-07-20T13:59:30.335Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=e777b63038820381ca4b383c2a112a16]   Todo:      The chat panel's AI response and generation checklist are static placeholders; actual AI generation, streaming, and a live preview still need to be wired up. The authenticated project-creation flow (sign in, submit a prompt, land on the new project) has not yet been manually verified end-to-end since it requires real login credentials.
[2026-07-20T13:59:30.335Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=e777b63038820381ca4b383c2a112a16] Topic: Move page tabs from editor header into browser-style preview tabs
[2026-07-20T13:59:30.335Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=e777b63038820381ca4b383c2a112a16]   Trigger:   The user asked for the page tabs shown in the editor's top header (Home/Product/Collection) to be redesigned as actual browser-style tabs and relocated into the preview area, positioned to the right of the chat panel and below the header.
[2026-07-20T13:59:30.335Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=e777b63038820381ca4b383c2a112a16]   Response:  - Stripped the page-tab chips out of the editor header component, leaving it with only the logo, sidebar-collapse toggle, and the Export/Save actions.
- Built a new preview component (`components/editor/EditorPreview.tsx`) that renders a browser-like frame: a Chrome-style tab strip with closeable, addable page tabs, an address bar row with traffic-light dots, a domain/path display, a reload button, and desktop/tablet/mobile viewport toggles that resize the preview canvas.
- Updated the editor page to render this new preview component in place of the previous plain preview area, so the tabs now sit to the right of the chat panel, below the header.
[2026-07-20T13:59:30.335Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=e777b63038820381ca4b383c2a112a16]   Decisions: The tab and viewport controls were built as visual-only interactions for now (closing/adding tabs and switching viewport width work locally, but nothing is persisted or wired to real page generation yet), keeping this pass focused purely on matching the layout and interaction feel of the mockup before investing in the underlying page-management logic.
