[2026-07-20T21:41:35.820Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=8677c2cad75ee9b1716f67d838955cf1] === Summary content (3 topics) ===
[2026-07-20T21:41:35.820Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=8677c2cad75ee9b1716f67d838955cf1] Topic: Generated storefront images now created and served through ImageKit
[2026-07-20T21:41:35.820Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=8677c2cad75ee9b1716f67d838955cf1]   Trigger:   The user wanted every image on AI-generated storefront pages to come from ImageKit rather than static stock-photo placeholders, with a proper prompt generated for each image so ImageKit could produce a matching picture, and wanted a fallback shown if an image was slow to load.
[2026-07-20T21:41:35.820Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=8677c2cad75ee9b1716f67d838955cf1]   Response:  - Added URL-building helpers that construct ImageKit text-to-image links from a description and chain follow-on AI edits (background removal, upscaling, retouching, drop shadow, background replacement) onto an existing image URL, using only the public delivery endpoint so no private credentials reach the browser.
- Added an HTML rewriter that scans generated page markup for `<img>` tags carrying an image-description attribute (or a known placeholder host) and swaps their source for a freshly built ImageKit URL; this runs as the final step of the shared HTML-sanitizing pipeline so it applies uniformly to streaming previews, finished pages, patches, and inline edits.
- Updated the page-generation instructions so the AI model must attach a vivid photo description to every image element (plus width/height), and updated the preview shell to show a shimmering loading placeholder behind images while they load and swap in a neutral "image unavailable" graphic if loading fails or times out.
[2026-07-20T21:41:35.820Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=8677c2cad75ee9b1716f67d838955cf1]   Decisions: - **Single rewrite location over scattered patches**: hooking the ImageKit substitution into the one shared HTML-sanitizing function guarantees every code path (live streaming, saved pages, inline edits) gets consistent image handling without duplicating logic, at the cost of that function needing to stay side-effect-free and idempotent.
- **Public-endpoint-only, graceful degradation**: only the public delivery endpoint is ever sent to the browser, and when it isn't configured the rewriter is a no-op, so the builder keeps working with the original stock-photo placeholders rather than breaking when ImageKit isn't set up.
[2026-07-20T21:41:35.820Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=8677c2cad75ee9b1716f67d838955cf1] Topic: AI image generation and transform panel added to inline editing toolbar
[2026-07-20T21:41:35.820Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=8677c2cad75ee9b1716f67d838955cf1]   Trigger:   The user asked for the inline editing toolbar's image button to offer generating a new image and applying AI image transformations, selectable from the existing action popover.
[2026-07-20T21:41:35.820Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=8677c2cad75ee9b1716f67d838955cf1]   Response:  - Replaced the toolbar's old "AI image" button with a new ImageKit panel offering a prompt box to generate a brand-new image, one-click buttons for common AI transforms (remove background, upscale, retouch, drop shadow, variation), and prompt-driven options for background replacement and freeform AI edits.
- These actions build and mutate the image's delivery URL directly (chaining transform steps) rather than round-tripping through the AI backend, so changes apply instantly and are then saved as a normal edit revision.
- Added cleanup logic so transient loading/fallback markers added to images for the preview are stripped before the edited HTML is persisted, keeping saved markup clean.
[2026-07-20T21:41:35.820Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=8677c2cad75ee9b1716f67d838955cf1]   Decisions: Image transforms were implemented as direct, instant URL operations instead of new AI chat requests, trading a small amount of implementation complexity for near-instant feedback and avoiding extra AI-generation cost on every click.
[2026-07-20T21:41:35.820Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=8677c2cad75ee9b1716f67d838955cf1] Topic: Home page suggestion cards fill the prompt box instead of auto-submitting
[2026-07-20T21:41:35.820Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=8677c2cad75ee9b1716f67d838955cf1]   Trigger:   The user wanted clicking a suggestion card on the home page to insert its example prompt into the prompt box rather than immediately starting a new project.
[2026-07-20T21:41:35.820Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=8677c2cad75ee9b1716f67d838955cf1]   Response:  Updated the home page so clicking a suggestion card now sets the prompt text into the textarea and focuses it for further editing, instead of calling the project-start function directly; the arrow submit button and the keyboard shortcut still start the project exactly as before.
[2026-07-20T21:41:35.820Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=8677c2cad75ee9b1716f67d838955cf1]   Decisions: The change was scoped narrowly to the suggestion-card click handler so the existing manual-submit paths (button click and keyboard shortcut) stayed completely unchanged, minimizing risk while giving users a chance to review or tweak a suggested prompt before committing to it.
